## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where are you from?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Follow instructions for academic tasks or projects. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main plot and characters in short stories about changing schools and making new friends (e.g., "In the story, Gianluigi and Emma felt nervous about their first day at a new school, but they made new friends during lunch."). Can follow written instructions to complete school-related tasks or projects (e.g., "Step 1: Interview a classmate about their previous school. Step 2: Write three sentences comparing your schools."). Can recognize base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing) in texts about classmates and school activities (e.g., "Giacomo talks to his classmates every day." "Giuseppe played soccer after school." "Giulietta is studying for the test.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map or a map of Panama on the board. The teacher points to different cities/regions and asks questions like, "Where is Santiago?", "Is this your hometown?", "What do you know about this community?" Students briefly share what they know, activating vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., hometown, community, countryside, big, small).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to read a short descriptive text by reading aloud a paragraph about a new classmate from a different town, emphasizing fluent reading and clear pronunciation of words like 'new', 'friendly', 'nervous', and the endings of plurals/third person singular verbs (e.g., "He talks", "She plays"). The teacher clarifies that reading involves understanding the main idea and specific details.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I read a story about a new student, what kind of information will I find?" (Expected answer: Their name, where they come from, what they like, their previous school.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'main plot' mean in a story?" (Expected answer: The most important events, what the story is mostly about.)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[5-7 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (around 150-200 words) titled "Meet Our New Classmates: Gianluigi and Emma." The story describes Gianluigi, who moved from the countryside, and Emma, who moved from another city. They share feelings about their new school, describe their previous schools using comparatives (e.g., "bigger than," "smaller than"), and mention their new friends. It incorporates Present and Past Simple, comparatives, future tense (e.g., "I will join a club"), and target vocabulary (e.g., nervous, excited, friend, previous school, library, playground, cafeteria, learn, used to, come from, classmates, community, subject, activity).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Responding to questions about classmates and schools (e.g., "Where are you from?" "I'm from Santiago." "What was your previous school like?" "It was small but had a big library."). Comparing schools, classmates, or communities using comparatives and superlatives (e.g., "My new school is bigger than my old one." "She is the most talkative person in our class.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading for gist and specific information):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Reading (Gist):</b> Students read the story silently. Teacher asks, "What is the main idea of this story?" (Expected answer: It's about two new students at school.) "How do Gianluigi and Emma feel?" (Expected answer: Nervous but excited.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Reading (Specific Information):</b> Students read again. Teacher provides True/False statements based on the text. For example: "Gianluigi used to live in a big city. (False)", "Emma felt excited about making new friends. (True)".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match character names (Gianluigi, Emma) to descriptions of their previous schools or feelings. E.g., "Had a small library" (Gianluigi), "Used to walk to school" (Emma).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[10-12 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying Present and Past Simple, Comparatives, Future tense, and target vocabulary. Phonemic awareness of /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Vocabulary & Grammar Gap-fill:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with key vocabulary (e.g., nervous, friendly, community, previous) or grammar structures (e.g., used to, is bigger than) missing. Students read the story again to find the missing words and complete the sentences. Example: "Gianluigi felt very ____ on his first day." "Emma's old school ____ a bigger playground ____ her new one."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Transformation/Identification:</b> Students read specific paragraphs and identify all verbs in the Past Simple or sentences using comparatives. For instance, they might be asked to list three things Gianluigi 'used to' do at his old school or rewrite sentences to compare school features. Example: "Find two sentences where the characters compare their old and new schools."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Peer Reading:</b> The teacher highlights specific words from the story containing plurals or third-person singular verbs ending in -s, -es, or -ed (e.g., 'friends', 'talks', 'watches', 'walks', 'plays', 'teaches'). Students work in pairs to re-read these sentences aloud, focusing on correctly pronouncing the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds. They underline the base word and circle the inflectional ending. The teacher circulates, providing feedback and correction on pronunciation.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[15-18 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual "Classmate Profile Card" completion based on reading instructions and content from the story.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will receive a "New Classmate Profile Card" template. They must read the provided written instructions carefully to complete the profile for either Gianluigi or Emma, using information directly from the story. The instructions will include steps like: "Step 1: Choose one new classmate from the story (Gianluigi or Emma). Step 2: Read the story again to find details about their previous school and hometown. Step 3: Write their name, hometown, previous school, and two interesting facts about them (e.g., how they felt, what they used to do). Step 4: Add one sentence comparing their old school to their new school."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly reinforces identifying character information and following instructions related to new classmates and their backgrounds ("Where are you from?"), replicating a real-life school project.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for task completion and accuracy, and a short written comprehension check.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students submit their completed "New Classmate Profile Card".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students answer two multiple-choice questions or fill-in-the-blank questions based on the story, demonstrating overall comprehension of the main plot and characters. Example: "Which school was bigger, Gianluigi's old one or new one?" "What is Emma most excited about?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Accuracy of information provided on the profile card and answers to comprehension questions (main plot, characters).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Completeness of the profile card based on the given steps.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary & Grammar Use:</b> Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., previous, nervous, excited, friend) and grammatical structures (Present/Past Simple, comparatives) when completing the profile.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness (Observation during Practice):</b> Observable effort to pronounce inflectional endings correctly during peer reading.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two statements on a small slip of paper:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I learned that to understand a story well, I need to ____."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I remember from the story is ____."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[5 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the story was easy for you, and what was challenging?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding the main idea of a story help you in other subjects?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified the main plot and characters. The phonemic awareness activity for endings (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/) required more scaffolding, especially for the /ɪz/ sound. Students were engaged with the "Classmate Profile Card" task, showing good understanding of instructions. Next time, I will provide more explicit examples for identifying inflectional endings before the peer reading activity.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about new classmates. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact with their peers to share information about their own schools and classmates, using the same vocabulary and grammar in guided discussions and short interviews.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]<br>[3 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>